> I'm both reading and creating Usenet news articles in a browser window.
> That means Usenet and The Web are integrated at OSI level 7,
> presentation;

OSI level 7 is application.  Presentation is level 6.  If you're using
the Web, your application layer protocol is HTTP.  Even if you're using
Mailgate, everything is still coming and going between you and Mailgate
using HTTP.  Mailgate then moves the data between application layer
protocols.  How that data gets to Mailgate is independent of what can be
done with it.  They could decide to use FTP or telnet if they felt like
it.  That wouldn't make FTP or telnet NNTP.  They're not integrated.  

> I logged in to my news provider right from my browser
> window, so they are integrated at level 6, session; 

Level 6 is presentation.  Session is level 5.  Integration on these
levels isn't exactly a good determining factor for similarity.
Presentation only cares about syntax, compression, encryption, and data
security.  Similarly, session only cares about connection creation,
maintenance, and synchronization.  Everything else is immaterial.  

> they are also integrated at least at levels 1, 2, & 3, sharing common
> transport and physical medium. 

Again, this means even less than sharing levels 5 and 6.  The actual
contents are moot at these levels.  

> If they bifurcate to make a lattice somewhere in between, that is an
> implementation detail not visible to the average user in this mode.  I
> never claimed they were identical, just mingled;

You also claimed they were indistinct.  I certainly accept that for some
people they're intermingled from the user perspective, but that's all.
Beyond that, they're completely distinct.  If you're using a web browser
to look at Mailgate, you aren't accessing some strange HTTP/NNTP hybrid.
You're using HTTP, pure and simple.  

> if you use some old offshoot to access Usenet exclusive of the WEB,
> that's a choice, it doesn't invalidate that they have been integrated,
> and are available integrated to anyone who cares to use them that way.

If they had been truly integrated, they'd exist as a single whole.  I
certainly accept that one can add another layer of UI and make them
appear to be intermingled, but that's all.  

